    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 212011\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n110 PM MST Mon Sep 21 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dry and tranquil conditions, along with near normal temperatures \nwill continue through at least the middle of the week.\n\n- Rain chances will return to portions of Arizona over the next few \ndays, although  focused primarily over foothill and high terrain \nareas.\n\n- Lower desert rainfall chances will be heightened somewhat during \nthe latter half of the week. \n  \n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /Today through Wednesday/...\nA seasonally strong trough was moving onshore in southern California \nearly this afternoon with a jet streak translating downstream of the \ncold core forcing deamplification and lifting of the cyclonic \ncirculation towards the Great Basin. However, jet forced ascent has \ncombined with a narrow band of midlevel moisture to produce a small \narea of elevated convection along the lower Colorado River valley \nearlier today. This feature will continue to exit the forecast area \nby this evening although residual, elongated troughing will linger \nacross southern California/Nevada. This will create a deeply \nmeridional flow pattern over the next 72 hours capable of importing \nsheared vorticity centers from the south, as well as periodically \nbetter low level moisture profiles. H5 heights will remain nearly \nsteady state in a 580-584dm range ensuring a continuation of near \nnormal temperatures the next several days. \n\nBoth global and HREF modeling suggests a low amplitude vorticity \ncenter (likely convectively enhanced over northern Mexico) streaming \ninto eastern Arizona Tuesday afternoon. Along with a small jet \nsegment feeding into the right rear quadrant of the northern stream, \nascent structure tapping marginally better moisture in SE Arizona \nwill become very favorable for deep convective development over \nhigher terrain locations. While lower elevations in the CWA will not \ninitially have sufficient moisture for thunderstorm sustenance, \ngusty southeast outflow winds seem more likely, fostered by DCape \nvalues near 1500 J/kg allowing better boundary layer moisture to \nsurge northwest through the forecast area. As a result, instability \nparameters will reach much higher levels Wednesday (i.e. MLCape 1000-\n1500 J/kg), though stronger CinH and lack of a triggering mechanism \nmay preclude more than isolated mountain storms as the region may \nfall under shortwave ridging and subsidence behind the Tuesday \nvorticity center exiting the region. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /Thursday through Sunday/... \nBy the latter half of the week, the influence of the weakening \nsouthwestern low will all but disappear. The focus will shift toward \nthe interplay between large scale troughing over the eastern \nPacific/West Coast and the subtropical high over the Southern Plains \nand Northern Mexico. Global ensembles favor retrogression toward the \nDesert Southwest approaching the weekend. This evolution would not \ncompletely scour residual moisture immediately, so rain chances will \nlikely linger into Thursday. Current PoPs show 30-50% chances for \nfoothill and mountain areas and 10-30% probabilities for the South-\nCentral Arizona deserts. Beyond that, persistent southwesterly flow \nwould eventually dry us out enough to limit rain chances heading \ninto the weekend. Another influx of moisture by the end of the seven \nday period may bring rain chances back once again, but max chances \nat this time only range between 20-30%. \n  \nEven with the competing airmasses, regional temperatures will only \nfluctuate within a few degrees through the weekend. While the ridge \nkeeps another shortwave from progressing toward the region, as \nmentioned previously, in return, the large scale troughing to our \nwest will keep the high from becoming too amplified, preventing any \nfurther discussion of abnormal heat, at least during this forecast \nperiod. Temperatures for the lower deserts Thursday through Sunday \nwill range generally between 94-106 degrees, while overnight lows \nhover near 70-82 degrees. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1740Z. \n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nNo major aviation weather concerns will exist through Tuesday \nafternoon under periods of SCT-BKN mid and high cloud decks with \nbases remaining AOA 8K ft AGL. Winds will veer out of the SSE/S and \nmaintain this direction through much of the afternoon, presenting a \nminor concern with a southerly cross-runway component at KPHX and \nKDVT. However, only occasional gusts to around 15 kts are expected \nthrough the afternoon with speeds remaining mostly AOB 10 kts. By \nthis evening, a much later than usual westerly wind switch is \nanticipated. A familiar pattern will take shape tonight and Tuesday \nmorning, with E/SE nocturnal drainage winds establishing followed by \nwinds veering out of the SSE Tuesday morning. Chances for SHRA/TSRA \nin the Phoenix airspace remain below 10% through Tuesday afternoon, \nwith most activity remaining well to the east and southeast, \nhowever, some periods with virga showers in the vicinity cannot be \nruled out. \n\n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH: \nSHRA/TSRA will clear KBLH within the next hour. Outside of this \nactivity, no major impacts are anticipated through the next 24 hours \nwith periods of SCT mid and cloud decks becoming clear by this \nevening. Winds will favor W/NW at KIPL, with a period of gusts to 20-\n25 kts expected this evening. At KBLH, winds will favor S through \nthe day with afternoon gusts between 15-20 kts likely, followed by \nSW-W winds this evening into the overnight hours.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nNear to slightly above normal temperatures will prevail over the \nnext several days, with minimal day-to-day fluctuations. Dry \nconditions with no chances for rain continue through today ahead \nreturning high terrain rain chances for foothill and high terrain \nareas starting Tuesday. MinRHs between 15-30% will be common across \nthe region, with even higher readings (35-50%) for the far eastern \ndistricts. MaxRHs will range from fair to good over the western and \ncentral districts, to good and excellent for the eastern districts \ninto the weekend. Outside of some marginal breeziness caused by an \napproaching weather system, winds will follow familiar diurnal \ntrends with typical terrain induced afternoon gusts around 15-25 \nmph. \n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...None.\nCA...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...18\nLONG TERM...RW \nAVIATION...Whittock\nFIRE WEATHER...RW\n",
